## Migration: `Thread.Abort` → Cooperative Cancellation (.NET 8)

### Pattern Identified: **Timeout Enforcement**

Your code uses `Thread.Abort()` to kill a thread exceeding a time limit. In .NET 6+, `Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` and `Thread.ResetAbort()` is also removed (`SYSLIB0006`).

### What Changes and Why

| .NET Framework | .NET 8 Replacement | Reason |
|---|---|---|
| `Thread.Abort()` | `CancellationTokenSource.CancelAfter()` | Cooperative cancellation replaces preemptive abort |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` | Different exception type for cancellation |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Removed entirely | No abort to reset — cancellation is checked voluntarily |
| `new Thread(() => ...)` | `Task.Run(..., token)` | Task-based model integrates with cancellation natively |

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        var token = cts.Token;

        try
        {
            var task = Task.Run(() => work(), token);
            return task.WaitAsync(token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }

    // Preferred: async overload
    public async Task<T> ExecuteWithTimeoutAsync<T>(
        Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout, CancellationToken externalToken = default)
    {
        using var cts = CancellationTokenSource
            .CreateLinkedTokenSource(externalToken);
        cts.CancelAfter(timeout);

        try
        {
            return await Task.Run(() => work(), cts.Token)
                             .WaitAsync(cts.Token);
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (!externalToken.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
        // If externalToken caused cancellation, OperationCanceledException propagates
    }
}
```

### Key Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Detail |
|---|---|
| **Cancellation is cooperative, not preemptive** | Unlike `Thread.Abort`, cancellation only works if `work()` checks the token. If `work()` is a tight CPU loop that never checks, it will **not** stop. Pass the token into `work` and call `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` at regular checkpoints. |
| **`Task.Run` + cancellation ≠ thread kill** | The task's body keeps running until it reaches a cancellation checkpoint. `WaitAsync` unblocks the *caller* at timeout, but the work may continue in the background. |
| **Dispose `CancellationTokenSource`** | `CTS` leaks internal timers if not disposed. Always use `using`. |
| **Don't swallow `OperationCanceledException`** | The `when` filter distinguishes timeout vs. external cancellation — only convert to `TimeoutException` when *your* CTS triggered it. |
| **Blocking I/O won't respond to cancellation** | Replace synchronous `Stream.Read()`, `Socket.Receive()`, `Thread.Sleep()` in the work delegate with async overloads that accept a `CancellationToken`. |

### If `work()` Cannot Be Modified (Uncooperative Code)

If the function ignores cancellation (third-party library, native call), `CancellationToken` alone won't stop it. Move the work to a **child process** and use `Process.Kill()`:

```csharp
// Last resort for truly uncooperative code
var process = Process.Start("worker.exe", args);
if (!process.WaitForExit(timeout))
{
    process.Kill(entireProcessTree: true);
    throw new TimeoutException("...");
}
```

This is the only safe equivalent to the old `Thread.Abort` force-stop behavior.
